   "text": "Review of `portal/portal_payment.php`. Issues are ordered by severity.\n\n**Critical / High**\n\n1. **Gateway secret exposed to the browser** (new `<script>` block: `var apiKey = json_encode($GLOBALS['gateway_api_key'])`)\n   - The globals metadata says this key is the \"API Secret for Stripe\" and the \"Auth Name\" for Authorize.Net.\n   - It is emitted into the page for every portal user, whatever the gateway. With Stripe this leaks the secret key, which allows charges and refunds against the merchant account.\n   - Only the publishable key should reach the client. Emit `apiKey` only for Authorize.Net, where the login ID is meant to be public, and never for Stripe.\n\n2. **IDOR on the new `recid` parameter** (`$recid` / `getPortalAuditRec($recid)`)\n   - `recid` is read straight from `$_REQUEST` and used to load a payment audit record by ID. There is no check that the record belongs to `$pid`, nor that the caller is staff. `$isPortal` is set but never used.\n   - A logged-in portal patient can iterate `recid` values and view other patients' decrypted card data and invoice data.\n   - This compounds the existing problem that `$pid` can be overridden by `$_REQUEST['pid']` and `hidden_patient_code` for portal sessions.\n   - Restrict `recid` to staff sessions, or require `audit.pid == $pid` for portal users. Also stop trusting request `pid` when `$isPortal` is true.\n\n3. **Stored XSS when staff view the audit record** (`echo $ccdata[\"cardNumber\"] . \"</span><br>\"`, and the `substr(...)` variant)\n   - `cardNumber` is user-supplied JSON that has been decrypted, and it is echoed with no escaping. It is displayed to staff, so this is a stored XSS against staff.\n   - Use `text()` as the other fields now do.\n\n4. **Card number can be posted to the server in Authorize.Net mode**\n   - The purpose of tokenization is to keep card data off the server. The Authorize.Net `paymentForm` is still a real `<form method='post' action='.../paylib.php'>`, and its fields (`cardNumber`, `cardCode`, `month`, `year`) have `name` attributes.\n   - Pressing Enter in a text field can natively submit the raw PAN and CVV to `paylib.php`. Clearing the fields before `fetch` does not help on the native submit path.\n   - Remove the `action`, add `onsubmit=\"return false\"`, or drop the `name` attributes on the sensitive inputs.\n\n5. **In-house mode stores and displays CVV and the full card number**\n   - Staff see `cardCode` and the unmasked number. Storing CVV, even encrypted, violates PCI-DSS.\n   - Not new in this PR, but the PR extends the feature. It at least needs a warning, or CVV should be dropped.\n\n**Medium**\n\n6. **Behavior change: `calendar_arrived($form_pid)` fallback removed.** Posts with `$enc == 0` now run queries and inserts against encounter 0. Confirm this is intentional and that the totals loop never emits a zero-encounter row.\n\n7. **Unauthorized amounts depend on the client.**\n   - `payment` is a hidden input populated from JS (`#payTotal` / `#paymentAmount`), and `invValues` is client-built JSON.\n   - `paylib.php` must recompute and validate the amount server-side against the invoice balances. This is not visible in the diff, so please confirm.\n\n8. **Stripe.js and Accept.js are not loaded anywhere in this diff.** `Stripe(publicKey)` and `Accept.dispatchData` will throw `ReferenceError` unless they are included elsewhere. Please verify.\n\n9. **Authorize.Net error path gives the user no feedback.** On `resultCode === \"Error\"` it only calls `console.log`, so the user clicks \"Pay Now\" and nothing happens.\n\n10. **Transactions without error handling.** `sqlBeginTrans()` and `SELECT MAX(sequence_no)+1` have no `FOR UPDATE`, so concurrent posts can still collide on `sequence_no`. There is also no rollback if the insert fails. The same block is copy-pasted three times and should be a helper.\n\n11. **Inconsistent user IDs.** The copay path uses `$_SESSION['authId']` for `ar_session.user_id` and `ar_activity.post_user`, while the other paths use `authUserID`. The old code also used a literal `'3'`. Pick one.\n\n12. **Event bindings may run before the DOM exists.** `$('#paySubmit').click(...)` and the new `$('#openPayModal').on('show.bs.modal', ...)` are bound in the head script. The removed `$(document).ready()` suggests they may not be wrapped. If so, the total is never copied into `#paymentAmount`, so the payment amount is blank. Wrap them in a `ready` handler.\n\n**Low / Maintainability**\n\n13. **Transaction ID display bug.** `text($ccdata[\"transId\"]) . \"/\" . text($ccdata[\"year\"])` appends the card expiry year to the transaction ID. This looks like a copy-paste error.\n\n14. **Malformed HTML.** `echo '<tr height=\"5\">...</tr><tr\">'` contains a stray quote in `<tr\">`.\n\n15. **Dead and redundant logic.** `if (option_id != 'credit_card' || option_id == 'debit' || ...) continue;` has redundant clauses, and swapping `electronic` for `debit` changes nothing.\n\n16. **Hard-coded year list (2019\u20132028).** The dropdown will go stale. Generate it with `date('Y')`.\n\n17. **Wrong HTML5 patterns.** `pattern=\"\\d\"` on the card number and zip fields only matches a single digit. It will fail native validation as soon as it is enforced. Use `\\d+` or `\\d{5}`.\n\n18. **Unused `$isPortal` variable.** See item 2, where it should be used.\n\n19. **Raw server response shown to the user.** `fetch(...).then(data => alert(data))` can display raw HTML or errors from the server.\n\n20. **Scope creep.** Mass reformatting (comment style, array wrapping) is mixed with security-relevant payment changes, which makes review harder."
